WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.620
The first film showed how integrins switch grip from inside and build force-sensing

2
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:13.900
adhesions. Yet a grip alone just anchors a cell, so how do switches shape actin to

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.420
make it crawl? Three small switches of the Rho family run the actin skeleton.

4
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:25.333
Like Ras, Rho, Rac and Cdc42 flip between two states.

5
00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:30.633
Bound to GTP, a switch is on and engages its effectors.

6
00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:33.653
Bound to GDP, it is off.

7
00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:39.273
Exchange factors swap GDP for GTP to turn a switch on.

8
00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:46.093
GTPase-activating proteins speed its own GTP splitting, which turns it off.

9
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:52.533
Switch Rho on in a resting fibroblast, and thick contractile cables span the cell.

10
00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:59.353
These are stress fibres, bundles of actin and myosin II anchored at focal adhesions.

11
00:01:00.813 --> 00:01:06.613
Rho activates Rho kinase, which raises myosin II activity so the cables contract and

12
00:01:06.613 --> 00:01:07.613
pull.

13
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.227
Now switch to Rac.

14
00:01:10.887 --> 00:01:15.387
Active Rac pushes out a broad, thin sheet at the cell edge called a lamellipodium.

15
00:01:16.887 --> 00:01:20.977
Rac turns on the WAVE complex, which activates Arp2/3.

16
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:26.267
Arp2/3 starts each new actin filament as a branch on an older one.

17
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.100
Third comes Cdc42.

18
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.300
It is best known for triggering filopodia, thin fingers with cores of parallel

19
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.420
filaments that probe the surface ahead.

20
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.080
Other proteins, mainly formins, build those parallel cores.

21
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:49.700
Separately, Cdc42 opens folded WASP-family proteins, freeing them to activate

22
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.700
Arp2/3.

23
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:54.303
Adhesions and switches are wired together.

24
00:01:55.783 --> 00:02:01.823
Proteins phosphorylated by focal adhesion kinase and Src recruit exchange factors

25
00:02:01.823 --> 00:02:07.483
that turn Rac on. A fresh grip at the front therefore calls for more pushing there.

26
00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:12.823
Put the steps in order: push with Rac, grip with integrins, pull with Rho.

27
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:15.557
One step is still missing.

28
00:02:16.817 --> 00:02:21.177
Suppose a crawling cell locks its integrins tightly at both front and back.

29
00:02:22.457 --> 00:02:24.557
None of its old adhesions can come apart.

30
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:29.277
When Rho and myosin II contract the cell body, what happens?

31
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:36.873
The cell stalls in place.

32
00:02:38.193 --> 00:02:43.053
To move forward, old adhesions at the back must come apart so the tail draws in.

33
00:02:44.393 --> 00:02:47.413
Push, grip, pull, let go: that cycle moves the cell.

34
00:02:48.873 --> 00:02:51.633
Rac in front and Rho behind is a simplification.

35
00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:56.933
Sensors of Rho activity in living cells find some at the front too.

36
00:02:57.967 --> 00:03:02.907
Rho, Rac and Cdc42 shape actin to turn grip into crawling.

37
00:03:04.127 --> 00:03:10.827
Rac pushes broad sheets, Cdc42 probes with fingers, and Rho contracts cables against

38
00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:15.787
adhesions. Break the grip or the switches, and this kind of crawling stalls.
